WEBVTT

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:12.270
<v Ira Glass>When she was seven, when she would visit her grandmother, Alexa would look through the books that her grandfather had owned back when he was alive. What she liked especially was finding the books where he'd made little notes in the margins.
<v Ira Glass>Alexa七岁时，每当去祖母家，她都会翻看祖父生前拥有的那些书。她特别喜欢找那些他在书页空白处留有小笔记的书。

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:16.100
<v Alexa Junge>So that was the part that was really compelling.
<v Alexa Junge>所以那部分内容真的很吸引人。

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.730
<v Ira Glass>Because they were hints about who he was.
<v Ira Glass>因为那是关于他为人的线索。

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:35.110
<v Alexa Junge>Exactly. And a lot of times they were really critical. He would write, I steadfastly disagree. Or something like that. Or he would write, Ah!, if he really liked something.
<v Alexa Junge>没错。而且很多时候他的评价非常尖刻。他会写下“我坚决反对”，或者类似的话。如果他真的很喜欢什么，他就会写“啊！”。

00:00:35.110 --> 00:01:00.790
<v Ira Glass>As a kid, over the course of about a year, she systematically divided the books into two piles, the ones with markings, and the ones without. And then she tried to read all the ones with markings. Her grandfather was a playwright and a teacher, and the books were creaky old books from the 1930s about theater and about how to write plays. It was thrilling. And when she was 11, she wrote her very first play, using the rules in the books, rules from another generation.
<v Ira Glass>小时候，在大约一年的时间里，她系统地把这些书分成了两堆：有批注的和没批注的。然后她试着读完了所有有批注的书。她的祖父是一位剧作家兼老师，这些书是20世纪30年代关于戏剧和剧本写作的陈旧书籍，读起来嘎吱作响。这太令人兴奋了。11岁时，她根据书中的规则——那些来自上一代人的规则——写出了她的第一部剧本。

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:28.700
<v Alexa Junge>These were archaic rules, like start your play with lots of exposition, which was really in vogue at the time. So I started mine with a butler, whose name I believe was Manson, picking up a phone, saying stuff like, no, the lady and gentleman are not home right now. Why, at a fancy charity ball. Yes, he's still drinking too much, and she's having an affair with a gardener. Whom shall I say is calling?
<v Alexa Junge>那是一些老派的规则，比如让你的剧本以大量的背景介绍开头，这在当时非常流行。所以我用了个管家开头，我记得他叫曼森，他接起电话说，不，先生和太太现在不在家。哎呀，在一个高级慈善舞会上。是的，他还是喝得太多了，而她正和园丁有染。请问是谁在打电话？

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.360
<v Alexa Junge>[LAUGHTER]
<v Alexa Junge>[笑声]

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:31.160
<v Alexa Junge>I'm not kidding.
<v Alexa Junge>我没开玩笑。

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:36.460
<v Ira Glass>You were 11.
<v Ira Glass>你才11岁。

00:01:36.460 --> 00:02:09.440
<v Alexa Junge>By the time I got to college and I started to actually take writing classes, it was brought to my attention that stage directions shouldn't be things like, there follows a mighty howling of wind. And one of the things my teacher-- who was not a young man, by any means-- said was, he was like, sweetheart, we don't use sotto voce anymore to mean he whispers. We just write, whispers.
<v Alexa Junge>当我上大学并开始真正上写作课时，我注意到舞台提示不应该是“随后传来一阵强烈的风啸声”这样的东西。我的老师——他绝对不是个年轻人——说，亲爱的，我们不再用“sotto voce”（轻声地）来表示耳语了。我们直接写“耳语”就行。

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:16.630
<v Ira Glass>But of all the books on her grandfather's shelves, there was one book that affected her more than the others. It had lots of her grandfather's writing in the margins.
<v Ira Glass>但在祖父书架上的所有书中，有一本书对她的影响超过了其他任何一本。书页空白处写满了她祖父的笔迹。

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:34.760
<v Alexa Junge>And he was very critical, so it was very rare that he would write, Ah!, exclamation point. And there were more Ahs! in Moss Hart's autobiography, which is called Act One , then I think almost any of the other books that he had.
<v Alexa Junge>他非常挑剔，所以他写下“啊！”（后面还加个感叹号）是非常罕见的。而在莫斯·哈特的自传《第一幕》中，出现的“啊！”比他收藏的其他任何书都要多。

00:02:34.760 --> 00:03:02.910
<v Ira Glass>Moss Hart was a Broadway playwright, the man who directed My Fair Lady with Julie Andrews and Rex Harrison, who was married to another then-luminary named Kitty Carlisle, who people these days mostly remember as a game show panelist back in the 1960s. The book details how he started as a kid in the Bronx, found something he just loved to do, which was to make plays. Reading it as a child, Alexa had that experience that you have sometimes as a kid. She did not understand everything in the book, but she understood enough to know that she really, really liked it.
<v Ira Glass>莫斯·哈特是一位百老汇剧作家，曾执导朱莉·安德鲁斯和雷克斯·哈里森主演的《窈窕淑女》，他的妻子是当时另一位名流基蒂·卡莱尔，而如今人们大多只记得她是20世纪60年代的游戏节目嘉宾。这本书详细记录了他如何从布朗克斯的一个孩子开始，找到了他真正热爱的事情——编剧。儿时的Alexa阅读此书时，产生了一种孩子们有时会有的体验。她并不能理解书中所有的内容，但她理解得足够多，知道自己非常、非常喜欢它。

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:10.490
<v Alexa Junge>Like, I knew what was going on and this book was fun. It drove him so powerfully, and it seemed to make him so happy.
<v Alexa Junge>就像，我知道发生了什么，而且这本书很有趣。它极大地驱动着他，似乎让他非常快乐。

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:21.590
<v Ira Glass>She read Act One by Moss Hart over and over. She memorized long stretches. She tried to memorize the entire book. Even today, she recalls where specific Ahs! were penciled into her grandfather's copy.
<v Ira Glass>她一遍又一遍地读莫斯·哈特的《第一幕》。她背下了很长的段落。她甚至试图背下整本书。直到今天，她还记得祖父那本书里具体的“啊！”都记在哪些地方。

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:33.530
<v Alexa Junge>Because it felt like I was recognizing an old friend. It felt like a familiarity of, oh, I found a home. This guy likes the same home I want. So--
<v Alexa Junge>因为它让我感觉像是在认出一位老朋友。那种熟悉感就像是，哦，我找到了家。这家伙喜欢和我想要的同一个家。所以——

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:34.320
<v Ira Glass>These are my people.
<v Ira Glass>这些人是我的同类。

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.080
<v Alexa Junge>Yeah, yeah.
<v Alexa Junge>是啊，是啊。

00:03:40.080 --> 00:04:04.210
<v Ira Glass>You don't meet many people who tell you that a book changed their lives. It's an appealing notion, I think, because it's nice to think that our lives could be changed just by an idea, by the vision of the world that happens in a book, instead of what our lives are often changed by-- you know, dumb luck, tragedies, coincidences. Today on our radio program, stories of people whose lives were changed by books.
<v Ira Glass>你遇不到多少人会告诉你一本书改变了他们的人生。我认为这是一个很有吸引力的念头，因为想到我们的人生能被一个想法、被一本书中对世界的愿景所改变，而不是被那些我们常被其改变的东西——你知道，走运、悲剧、巧合——所驱动，这感觉很好。今天在我们的广播节目中，讲述的是那些人生被书籍改变的人们的故事。

00:04:04.210 --> 00:05:07.560
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Our program today in four acts. Act One is called, well, Act One, getting clues about how to live your life from notes scribbled by your dead grandfather in the margins of a book. Act Two, The Family That Reads Together. In this act, the story of how when David Sedaris was a boy, he stumbled upon a dirty book in the woods. It made his sisters view all adults with newfound suspicion. It sent him to the dictionary. Act Three, Roger and Me, and Lewis and Clark, the story of a construction worker, and this question, can your life be changed by a book that you have never seen and have not read? Act Four, Little Sod Houses For You and Me. It is the old, old story, my friend. New York girl leaves big city, heads out to a small town on the prairie with a dream and a bonnet. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自WBEZ芝加哥和国际公共广播电台的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们今天的节目分为四幕。第一幕叫作《第一幕》，内容是从你已故祖父在书页空白处的潦草笔记中，获取关于如何过好人生的线索。第二幕，《爱读书的家庭》。在这一幕里，讲述大卫·塞达里斯小时候在树林里偶然发现一本黄色小说的故事。这让他的姐妹们开始以一种新的怀疑眼光看待所有成年人。这还让他去翻阅了字典。第三幕，《罗杰和我，以及刘易斯和克拉克》，讲述一名建筑工人的故事，以及这样一个问题：你的人生会被一本你从未见过、也未读过的书改变吗？第四幕，《你我之间的小草房》。这是个古老的故事，朋友们。纽约女孩离开大城市，怀揣梦想和遮阳帽，前往草原上的小镇。请继续收听。

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:21.850
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Act One. So Alexa Junge says that she never meant for Moss Hart's autobiography to be a blueprint for her life, but looking back on the events of the past two decades, that seems to have been the case.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，《第一幕》。Alexa Junge说她从没想过莫斯·哈特的自传会成为她人生的蓝图，但回顾过去二十年的经历，情况似乎确实如此。

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:29.770
<v Alexa Junge>Basically, what I did was, like he did in his life story, I moved to New York. I think I kind of followed him there.
<v Alexa Junge>基本上，我做的就是，像他在人生故事里做的那样，我搬到了纽约。我想我算是追随着他来到这里的。

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:32.360
<v Ira Glass>Really? You consciously followed him there. You thought--
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？你是自觉地追随他来到这里的？你觉得——

00:05:32.360 --> 00:06:02.220
<v Alexa Junge>I don't think it was conscious, but there are so many things that I did that he did. I wasn't as good a-- I mean, he was more sort of-- He could fake it better than I can. But you know, he had to get money at a certain point. He was like, I need money. So he thought, who's the richest person I know? And he wrote a letter to this woman, I think, and then showed up on her doorstep and said, I'm Moss Hart and I have a play, and if you give me money, we'll put it on. And she did.
<v Alexa Junge>我不认为这是自觉的，但我做的很多事情和他做的一样。我没有他那么擅长——我是说，他更那种……他比我更会伪装。但你知道，他在某个时刻需要钱。他想，我认识的人里谁最有钱？于是他给那位女士写了封信，我想，然后出现在她家门口说，我是莫斯·哈特，我有个剧本，如果你给我投资，我们就把它排出来。然后她就给了。

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.090
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:13.410
<v Alexa Junge>Isn't that amazing? And I wrote letters to strangers and said, I'm Alexa and I have a play, and if you fund my play, you can be part of the theater.
<v Alexa Junge>这不神奇吗？我也给陌生人写信说，我是Alexa，我有个剧本，如果你为我的剧本提供资金，你就能参与到戏剧中来。

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:14.310
<v Ira Glass>And did that work?
<v Ira Glass>那成功了吗？

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:22.360
<v Alexa Junge>It did one time. Yeah.
<v Alexa Junge>有一次成功了。是的。

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:27.530
<v Ira Glass>At some point, did you start to get a crush on him?
<v Ira Glass>在某个时刻，你开始对他产生爱慕之情了吗？

00:06:27.530 --> 00:07:00.850
<v Alexa Junge>Yeah. It definitely turned from kind of a mentor, a make-believe mentor, to a pretend husband-to-be kind of situation. Yeah, somehow I think I decided that time had completely screwed up and sent Moss to Kitty Carlisle, and that if he just hadn't died two years before I was born, then me and Moss might really have had a chance.
<v Alexa Junge>是的。它确实从一种导师、假想的导师，变成了一种假装是未来丈夫的情况。是的，不知为何我想我认定是时间搞错了，把莫斯送给了基蒂·卡莱尔，如果他没有在我出生前两年去世，那么我和莫斯可能真的会有机会。

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:08.500
<v Ira Glass>How would this thought manifest itself in daily life? Like, would you be out on dates and just think, hmm, not Moss. He's not Moss.
<v Ira Glass>这种想法如何在日常生活中体现出来呢？比如，你会出去约会，然后心想，嗯，不是莫斯。他不是莫斯。

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:08.820
<v Alexa Junge>Yeah.
<v Alexa Junge>是的。

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:09.280
<v Ira Glass>Really?
<v Ira Glass>真的？

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:37.660
<v Alexa Junge>Yeah, yeah. Well, it would be like, there'd be something missing. It just wasn't quite what you'd want. And it was like, why can't I find some guy and we'll work on this play together and we'll be, like, in out-of-town tryouts in Philadelphia, and we'll be up for 48 hours trying to fix our play, and then we'll crack it, and then we'll order room service?
<v Alexa Junge>是啊，是啊。就会觉得，有些东西缺失了。它就是不像你想要的那样。就像是，为什么我找不到某个家伙和我一起打磨剧本，我们会在费城进行外地试演，为了修改剧本彻夜奋战48小时，然后我们成功了，最后再叫客房服务呢？

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:41.620
<v Ira Glass>So how far did the whole thing go?
<v Ira Glass>那整件事发展到了什么地步？

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:51.030
<v Alexa Junge>Well, I think, maybe right before the end of college, Kitty Carlisle spoke one evening.
<v Alexa Junge>嗯，我想，大概是在大学毕业前夕，基蒂·卡莱尔有天晚上发表了演讲。

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:52.280
<v Ira Glass>At your college?
<v Ira Glass>在你们大学？

00:07:52.280 --> 00:08:21.860
<v Alexa Junge>That's right. She was very active in the New York arts scene, and she was extremely a huge advocate of the arts in our country. And so she was talking about that, I think. And I stood in line after she spoke to meet her. And there were all these people around me, and they were like, you were really good on that game show. And I was just, like, disgusted, like, oh please, she was in A Night at the Opera. She's like a singer. She's not just a game show lady.
<v Alexa Junge>没错。她在纽约艺术界非常活跃，并且是国内艺术事业的极力倡导者。所以我想她当时在谈论那个。演讲结束后，我排队去见她。周围都是人，他们说，您在那个游戏节目里表现得真好。我当时觉得，厌恶，就像，拜托，她出演过《歌剧院之夜》。她是一位歌手，不只是个游戏节目的女士。

00:08:21.860 --> 00:09:02.010
<v Alexa Junge>But by the time I got to the front of the line, and I went up to talk to her, I said what I wanted to say, sort of, which was, you know, Moss changed my life and I moved to New York to be a playwright like him. And I think I said something along the lines of, your husband meant so much to me. And she just looked at me, and she was so elegant and so classy, and she just said, I don't understand, darling. Did you know him? She was just terrified.
<v Alexa Junge>但当我排到队伍前面去跟她交谈时，我说了我想说的话，或者说类似的话，那就是，你知道，莫斯改变了我的人生，我搬到纽约就是为了像他一样成为一名剧作家。我想我大概说了类似于“你的丈夫对我意义重大”这样的话。她看着我，她是那么优雅、那么有品位，她只是说，我不明白，亲爱的。你认识他吗？她被吓到了。

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:03.340
<v Ira Glass>Like, really? She looked terrified?
<v Ira Glass>像，真的吗？她看起来很害怕？

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:26.830
<v Alexa Junge>Yeah. I think she probably heard some kind of ownership or possessiveness in the way I said your husband meant so much to me, as if I knew him. So I think it was confusing, since she probably could figure out that he probably was dead before I was born.
<v Alexa Junge>是的。我想她大概从我说“你的丈夫对我意义重大”的方式里听出了一种归属感或占有欲，好像我认识他一样。所以我认为这很令人困惑，因为她大概能推断出他可能在我出生前就去世了。

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:50.180
<v Alexa Junge>But it was disturbing, and I felt terrible. And it made me realize how just far from reality this thing had taken me. And it was just scary to scare her, because she's the person that he loved.
<v Alexa Junge>但这很令人不安，我感到糟透了。它让我意识到这件事已经让我偏离现实多远了。吓到她是件很可怕的事，因为她是那个他所爱的人。

00:09:50.180 --> 00:10:00.380
<v Alexa Junge>But my friend that was with me was really nice, because we walked home afterward, and he was like, eh, don't worry about her. You're much better for Moss than she was. He knew the whole story, too.
<v Alexa Junge>但我同行的朋友真的很贴心，因为我们后来走路回家时，他说，诶，别担心她。你比她更适合莫斯。他也知道整个故事。

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:33.580
<v Ira Glass>Eh, Moss was just spending time with her because she happened to be alive. You know, you talked about how you felt fated for him in some way, and drawn to him in some way. Have you thought about what is the line that divides that kind of dreamy, healthy feeling, I think, from a scary, stalky feeling?
<v Ira Glass>诶，莫斯只是因为她恰好活着才和她在一起。你知道，你谈到你觉得以某种方式与他命中注定，并被他吸引。你有没有想过，什么才是区分那种梦幻、健康的感受和可怕的跟踪狂感觉的分界线？

00:10:33.580 --> 00:11:05.400
<v Alexa Junge>Yeah sure, because the truth is, really, the way he functioned in my life was like as a comfort. And I knew. I mean, it wasn't a break from reality, but it was the sense that, when you read a book and something speaks to you and you feel understood, and so it makes the world a less lonely place.
<v Alexa Junge>当然，因为事实是，他在我生命中发挥的作用确实是一种慰藉。我知道。我的意思是，这并不是脱离现实，而是一种感觉：当你读一本书，有些东西触动了你，你觉得被理解了，所以这让世界变得不再那么孤独。

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:14.970
<v Ira Glass>Alexa, how much of your feeling about Moss is connected to your feeling about your grandfather, who you didn't really know?
<v Ira Glass>Alexa，你对莫斯的感情有多少是和你对你祖父的感情联系在一起的？毕竟你并不是很了解他。

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:51.670
<v Alexa Junge>I think they're intimately connected. I really do. I think that, because I didn't know my grandfather, I couldn't talk to him about what his life in the theater was like. And so this book gave me 444 pages of what it would be like to want to be in the theater and how you might try to make that happen. And so it was like he was the sort of stand-in for my grandfather in a lot of ways.
<v Alexa Junge>我想它们是紧密相连的。真的。我认为，因为我不了解我的祖父，我没法和他谈论他在戏剧界的生活是什么样的。所以这本书给了我444页关于想要进入戏剧界会是什么样子，以及你可能如何尝试去实现它的内容。所以从很多方面来看，他就像是我祖父的替身。

00:11:51.670 --> 00:12:30.880
<v Alexa Junge>And the other part about it is that the way people talk about him-- because I then, of course, went and read every single book I could get my hands on about him, or that even had any mention of him in it-- is with such love and appreciation and affection. It's just staggering. I think that that was how people spoke about my grandfather. And I recognized it, that same enthusiasm and sort of the way their eyes would light up.
<v Alexa Junge>另一部分是，人们谈论他的方式——因为我后来当然跑去读了所有我能找到的关于他的书，甚至是任何提到过他的书——那是带着如此多的爱、欣赏和感情。简直令人震惊。我认为这就是人们谈论我祖父的方式。我认出来了，那是同样的狂热，以及人们谈起他时眼神亮起来的样子。

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:55.770
<v Ira Glass>Today, before our interview, you faxed over to our offices here at WBEZ a-- I'm going to pull it out here-- a letter that your grandfather wrote in 1969, obviously as he was quite ill. And it does have this quality of just-- It's one of the most beautifully written things I've ever read.
<v Ira Glass>今天，在我们采访前，你传真到我们WBEZ办公室一封——我把它拿出来——一封你祖父在1969年写下的信，显然当时他病得很重。它有一种……这是我读过写得最美的文章之一。

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:59.210
<v Alexa Junge>Isn't it? It's really something.
<v Alexa Junge>是吗？确实很有分量。

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:03.600
<v Ira Glass>He spends, I should say most of this thing, he starts off-- this is to his students, right?
<v Ira Glass>他花了，我应该说这封信的大部分，他开头写着——这是写给他的学生的，对吧？

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:04.770
<v Alexa Junge>Mmm-hmm. yep.
<v Alexa Junge>嗯，是的。

